# Mahabharata – A Simple Story

Long ago, in the Kuru kingdom, there lived two groups of cousins called the Pandavas and the Kauravas. The five Pandavas were Yudhishthira, Bhima, Arjuna, Nakula, and Sahadeva. The Kauravas were one hundred brothers led by Duryodhana.

As children, both groups learned archery, sword fighting, and other skills from their teacher, Guru Drona. Arjuna became the greatest archer, while Duryodhana grew jealous of the Pandavas because everyone admired them.

When the Pandavas built the beautiful city of Indraprastha, their kingdom became rich and peaceful. Duryodhana could not bear their success. With the help of his uncle Shakuni, he invited Yudhishthira to a game of dice. Shakuni cheated, and Yudhishthira lost his kingdom, his wealth, and even the freedom of his brothers.

The Pandavas were forced to spend twelve years in the forest and one year living in disguise. After completing their exile, they peacefully asked for their kingdom to be returned. However, Duryodhana refused to give them even a small piece of land.

Lord Krishna tried to make peace between the two families, but his efforts failed. As a result, the great battle of Kurukshetra began. Before the war, Arjuna became sad because he had to fight his own relatives and teachers. Krishna encouraged him by teaching the Bhagavad Gita, explaining that everyone must perform their duty with honesty and courage.

The war lasted for eighteen days. Many brave warriors, including Bhishma, Drona, and Karna, fought with great courage. In the end, Bhima defeated Duryodhana, and the Pandavas won the war. Although they were victorious, they were heartbroken because many good people had lost their lives.

Yudhishthira became the king and ruled with justice, kindness, and honesty. His kingdom prospered, and people lived in peace. After many years, the Pandavas handed over the kingdom to the next generation and began their final journey toward the Himalayas. Yudhishthira, because of his truthfulness and righteousness, finally reached heaven.



The Mahabharata teaches us that truth, justice, courage, and kindness always lead to victory, while greed, pride, and dishonesty bring sorrow and defeat.

